WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.610
Instructor: The last thing we'll talk about here is

00:02.610 --> 00:05.850
in 2020, I've mentioned this a few times, GitHub decided

00:05.850 --> 00:10.290
to rename the default branch from master to main.

00:10.290 --> 00:14.040
Now we've seen that that doesn't really impact us, right?

00:14.040 --> 00:16.050
We've been able to just push up a master branch

00:16.050 --> 00:18.600
and it's defaulting here to showing us master.

00:18.600 --> 00:22.020
If I just go to this repo, we see master.

00:22.020 --> 00:23.970
However, I do wanna talk about it.

00:23.970 --> 00:25.800
If we make a brand new repository,

00:25.800 --> 00:28.740
we'll just call this, how about colors?

00:28.740 --> 00:31.470
All right, here's our repo called colors.

00:31.470 --> 00:32.640
There it is, colors.

00:32.640 --> 00:35.370
And I'm gonna create this repository

00:35.370 --> 00:38.760
and maybe this time I'm gonna initialize it with something.

00:38.760 --> 00:40.590
So we will learn about readmes

00:40.590 --> 00:42.930
but I'm just gonna click this box.

00:42.930 --> 00:45.180
And notice it tells me something different now, right?

00:45.180 --> 00:47.610
It says this will set main as the default branch.

00:47.610 --> 00:51.270
If I get rid of that, it doesn't say anything about main

00:51.270 --> 00:53.220
because there is no branch.

00:53.220 --> 00:57.510
So when I create this repo as is with no readme,

00:57.510 --> 00:59.280
just completely empty,

00:59.280 --> 01:01.800
there is no branch that is initialized.

01:01.800 --> 01:04.143
But as soon as I add a file to GitHub,

01:05.550 --> 01:08.490
when I'm initializing this repository is what I mean,

01:08.490 --> 01:11.040
it automatically needs to have a branch, right?

01:11.040 --> 01:14.340
You can't have files in a Git repo that are tracked

01:14.340 --> 01:17.250
and committed if they're not associated with some branch.

01:17.250 --> 01:20.160
So it defaults that branch name to main.

01:20.160 --> 01:21.600
So we could change that

01:21.600 --> 01:24.240
in our settings so that it always defaults to master.

01:24.240 --> 01:27.750
But you know, more and more people are using main anyway.

01:27.750 --> 01:29.070
And that's kind of the push

01:29.070 --> 01:31.920
that GitHub is working towards here.

01:31.920 --> 01:33.420
So if we do this,

01:33.420 --> 01:36.300
I create this repository, now it has a file in it.

01:36.300 --> 01:38.430
It has a commit, initial commit,

01:38.430 --> 01:39.660
that was made for me, right?

01:39.660 --> 01:43.290
I didn't say commit-m or anything like that.

01:43.290 --> 01:45.450
It just made me this readme file

01:45.450 --> 01:47.700
which is something we'll learn more about

01:47.700 --> 01:48.660
in future sections.

01:48.660 --> 01:50.460
So for now it's just a file.

01:50.460 --> 01:54.120
And I'm on the main branch.

01:54.120 --> 01:57.930
So in order to get this branch down to my machine, right?

01:57.930 --> 02:01.920
In order to work with it, I can clone this repository.

02:01.920 --> 02:05.340
So I'll copy that and then I'll go over here

02:05.340 --> 02:07.620
and make sure I'm not in a repo.

02:07.620 --> 02:09.363
Git clone, that URL.

02:10.800 --> 02:13.650
Okay? And then we have this colors repo, right?

02:13.650 --> 02:16.950
And if you look at the branch we're currently on, it's main.

02:16.950 --> 02:18.150
So this makes it easy.

02:18.150 --> 02:19.860
If we wanna work on the main branch,

02:19.860 --> 02:22.500
we just clone it and we have the main branch.

02:22.500 --> 02:26.520
But something that you may encounter is some companies

02:26.520 --> 02:29.280
or some projects may want to rename their branch

02:29.280 --> 02:31.650
from master to main.

02:31.650 --> 02:33.450
To be in accordance with these,

02:33.450 --> 02:35.490
they're not rules by any means from GitHub,

02:35.490 --> 02:38.096
but this new decision by GitHub.

02:38.096 --> 02:41.640
So to do that, if you've been working on the master branch

02:41.640 --> 02:43.860
and you want to rename it to main,

02:43.860 --> 02:46.140
it's just like renaming any other branch.

02:46.140 --> 02:48.450
So you can actually see instructions.

02:48.450 --> 02:51.540
If you make a new repository, I'm gonna delete this,

02:51.540 --> 02:54.090
but if we make a new repository, it shows us,

02:54.090 --> 02:56.160
as we've talked about a couple of times,

02:56.160 --> 02:57.330
what to do right?

02:57.330 --> 02:58.740
It gives you instructions.

02:58.740 --> 03:00.750
If you wanna wanna push an existing repository,

03:00.750 --> 03:04.260
add a remote and then this line renames the branch

03:04.260 --> 03:07.020
you're currently on to be main.

03:07.020 --> 03:09.180
Or, here's another version of this.

03:09.180 --> 03:11.040
If you don't have a repository yet,

03:11.040 --> 03:13.260
make a new repo, get an git init,

03:13.260 --> 03:16.860
add some stuff to it, commit, rename the branch to main,

03:16.860 --> 03:19.860
and then push your branch called main up.

03:19.860 --> 03:21.900
So I'll do this with our chickens demo.

03:21.900 --> 03:25.080
I have this master branch, but if I wanna rename it

03:25.080 --> 03:29.310
to main, whoopsies, I need to go to that repository.

03:29.310 --> 03:31.680
Geez, I can't type, back outta here.

03:31.680 --> 03:33.570
Cd into chickens, right?

03:33.570 --> 03:35.310
We have one branch, which is master.

03:35.310 --> 03:40.050
If I do git branch-M, main,

03:40.050 --> 03:43.170
that renames my branch to main.

03:43.170 --> 03:46.650
So now there is no master branch, right?

03:46.650 --> 03:48.060
We just have main.

03:48.060 --> 03:50.490
Remember, there is no significance

03:50.490 --> 03:53.100
to the master branch name itself

03:53.100 --> 03:54.540
or to the master branch period.

03:54.540 --> 03:57.630
It's just that you have to be working on a branch in Git.

03:57.630 --> 04:01.080
And by default, the the default branch name is master

04:01.080 --> 04:02.940
if you're working on Git on your machine.

04:02.940 --> 04:04.890
But on GitHub, the default branch name

04:04.890 --> 04:07.080
if you clone a new repo

04:07.080 --> 04:09.480
that you have not created a branch on

04:09.480 --> 04:12.810
but GitHub has made a branch, it will default to main.

04:12.810 --> 04:14.670
So don't get worried, don't freak out

04:14.670 --> 04:15.960
if you don't see master, right?

04:15.960 --> 04:18.690
It's this exact same concept, it's just a branch.

04:18.690 --> 04:20.340
And GitHub made this decision recently

04:20.340 --> 04:22.860
to make main that default name.

04:22.860 --> 04:27.813
So now I could push this branch up git push origin main.

04:30.030 --> 04:32.793
And if we head over here, if I refresh,

04:33.960 --> 04:35.910
we still see the master branch, right?

04:35.910 --> 04:38.100
But now we see main.

04:38.100 --> 04:39.164
So there's main.

04:39.164 --> 04:40.140
You'll see though

04:40.140 --> 04:43.350
that master is considered the default branch here.

04:43.350 --> 04:46.050
That is because we pushed up master first.

04:46.050 --> 04:47.520
There was no main branch.

04:47.520 --> 04:50.580
And so GitHub doesn't ignore a master branch.

04:50.580 --> 04:53.280
I can change with the default branch is on this project,

04:53.280 --> 04:55.260
but really you probably would not have main

04:55.260 --> 04:57.750
and master in the same project.

04:57.750 --> 05:01.050
What you would have is either a brand new empty project,

05:01.050 --> 05:02.250
you clone it down

05:02.250 --> 05:04.200
and you start on the main branch,

05:04.200 --> 05:07.680
or you work on a project with your company or some,

05:07.680 --> 05:08.513
you know, some team

05:08.513 --> 05:11.220
and they decide we're not gonna use the term master anymore.

05:11.220 --> 05:13.110
We will rename to main

05:13.110 --> 05:15.510
and then you can make main the default.

05:15.510 --> 05:20.040
I believe you go into settings, and then we have branches.

05:20.040 --> 05:20.880
And then I can decide

05:20.880 --> 05:23.823
that the default branch is actually main.

05:27.330 --> 05:30.780
And so now I've updated my project so that it defaults

05:30.780 --> 05:34.980
to showing me main when I visit my repository.

05:34.980 --> 05:38.070
So keep in mind through all of this, if you're confused,

05:38.070 --> 05:39.300
it's just a name.

05:39.300 --> 05:40.620
Master is just a name for a branch

05:40.620 --> 05:42.510
and main is just a name for a branch.

05:42.510 --> 05:45.330
The only complication comes if you have been working

05:45.330 --> 05:48.120
on one branch called master, and you change that name.

05:48.120 --> 05:50.700
If you change it locally, then you need to push up

05:50.700 --> 05:52.950
that new branch name to GitHub, and then

05:52.950 --> 05:55.530
if you care about what your default branch is on GitHub,

05:55.530 --> 05:57.090
you need to change that.

05:57.090 --> 06:00.060
But this is not, you know, some seismic shift.

06:00.060 --> 06:01.980
It's just a branch name that you can change

06:01.980 --> 06:02.970
and you'll likely see more

06:02.970 --> 06:05.610
and more people using main rather than master

06:05.610 --> 06:07.890
though master is still incredibly common

06:07.890 --> 06:10.233
and it's still the default branch name on Git.
